"""The Northern Barrens hub (the Gold Road, the Merchant Coast, the Stagnant Oasis, Far Watch, the Slug Fens, the
Sludge Fen, the Boulder Lode, Mor'shan, the Dry Hills and Serena's Hollow) in the plain voice (17 Nov 2026)."""

VOICE = {
    "nb_toll_collectors": (
        "I'm Hargak, and the waystation's mine, which means the road's my problem whether or not anyone's written that down.\n\nThe Gold Road is the only honest thing in the Northern Barrens. Everything else out here is a claim, a rumour or a goblin, and the road just goes where it says it goes. Someone's started charging for it. The Road Bandits wait at the bends. They're polite for exactly one sentence, a good sentence somebody taught them, and then every wagon that can't pay goes on lighter. They wear their coats turned the wrong way round so you can't tell who they are, but I can, because I've been looking at the backs of those coats for a season.\n\nThin them out along the bends. I've run out of patience, and I won't be getting it back.",
        None,
        "Fewer coats and more wagons, that's the sum I like.\n\nHere's what the waystation owes you.",
    ),
    "nb_the_highwaymans_ledger": (
        None,
        None,
        "They're going to hit the coast road next.\n\nI'd better tell someone with a spear.",
    ),
    "nb_cutthroats_on_the_road": (
        "The pages named a crew, and it isn't the one I thought I was fighting. There are cutthroats on foot and outriders on beasts, working the stretch between the oasis fork and the coast together, in rotation.\n\nThese aren't toll collectors. I've spent a season almost relieved that the road had bandits who asked first, because a toll collector wants you back next month with money. These ones don't ask. The Bandit Cutthroats move in pairs and the Bandit Outriders cover them from a distance, which is a soldier's arrangement and not a bandit's, and I've stopped wondering where they learned it.\n\nTake the outriders and their bows out first if you can manage the order. They hurt a lot more than they look like they should.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "nb_wreckers_beach": (
        "I'm Kizzle, the beachmaster, which is a title I gave myself and have successfully defended.\n\nThe Merchant Coast is this whole region's loading dock. Everything that comes into the Barrens by water comes in over my planks, and I'd like that to stay true. Every crab in the bay has decided it owns the pier. The Tide-Wrecker Crabs are big, red and sulky, and they pry the planks off the cargo crates and drag them out to sea, and nobody sees the planks or the crab again. I've got a theory involving a very large nest, and I've stopped sharing it.\n\nI asked nicely. I stood on the beach and did the whole thing. Now I'm asking differently. They hide in the surf. Crack a few, and the rest will remember they've got other places to be.",
        None,
        "Listen to that. There's nothing, not one claw!\n\nTake this, with the beachmaster's gratitude.",
    ),
    "nb_salvage_rights": (
        "This second problem has a legal side, and I'd like to walk you through it carefully. The Southsea Brigands have been dragging cargo crates up the beach and prying them open.\n\nAnything they've already opened is theirs, as a practical matter, because I'm not going door to door on a pirate beach with an inventory. But anything they haven't opened yet is still, strictly speaking, the Merchants' property. Nobody's ever reviewed my definition of strictly speaking, and it's never lost an argument.\n\nSo go and get the unopened ones, and don't open them. I know what's in them. Don't ask how.",
        None,
        "These are mine! I mean the Merchants', which is the same thing, really.\n\nTake this for your trouble.",
    ),
    "nb_silence_the_cannons": (
        "This one's urgent, and I'd like to get through it quickly, for reasons that'll become obvious. The Southsea crews have mounted cannons on the cliffs, and they're using them for target practice on anything that floats, which out here means my inbound shipping and the occasional confused whale.\n\nI've got a written guarantee, Ratchet-built and properly stamped, that this pier is definitely not in range. It's definitely in range. I've had the guarantee out twice this week and read it at the cliffs, and it hasn't helped.\n\nThe Southsea Cannoneers stand behind the brigands, so cut through the brigands to reach them. And whatever else you do up there, don't stand in a line.",
        None,
        "It's quiet again! Well, it's quiet for a harbour.\n\nHere, take this. It's cleaner than it looks.",
    ),
    "nb_captain_saltspray": (
        "This is the last one, and then you and I are going to sit on a crate and not talk about pirates. Captain Saltspray runs the Southsea crew.\n\nHe isn't good at it. I want that on the record, because everyone who hears the word captain assumes he's competent, and I've watched this man run a beach for two years. He's loud, and that's the whole of his gift. Loud is enough, because the cannoneers are brave when he's shouting and ordinary when he isn't.\n\nSo here's your fight. He only shows himself when his crew is winning. Kill enough of the Southsea Brigands that they stop winning, and then make him regret showing himself.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "nb_hounds_of_the_oasis": (
        "I'm Mahri, and I run the scouts out of Far Watch, and most of them are watching the Stagnant Oasis right now.\n\nThe Stagnant Oasis hasn't been fit to drink from in years, and everyone knows it. It still draws every wanderer on the plains, because people will walk toward water they've been told is bad. Lately it draws something else. The Dervish Hounds are lean, mangy things the Kolkar dervishes breed and set loose out here, not to guard anything, just to be loose.\n\nThey circle first and bite afterwards, and the circling is what gets people, because it goes on long enough that you start thinking about something else. Keep your back to something solid, and get rid of them before they work out what a campfire is for.",
        None,
        "They've stopped howling.\n\nI never realised how much of the night they were filling until they weren't.",
    ),
    "nb_curved_blades": (
        "Now for something stranger, and I'd like you to collect carefully rather than quickly. The Kolkar dervishes fight with curved blades, and they sharpen them on the same stones they pray at. I watched that for a month before I believed it.\n\nMy scouts want a handful of those blades, to read, not to use. The edges carry marks. Every dervish grinds a little differently, and every trainer passes his grind down, so a blade tells you who taught the man holding it, and who taught him. Out here, where nobody writes anything down, that's a family tree.\n\nCut them off the bodies, and don't clean them, because the grit on them is half the reading. Bring me enough to lay side by side.",
        None,
        "These all come from the same forge, every one of them.\n\nThere's a smith feeding the whole camp, and we didn't know that this morning.",
    ),
    "nb_stormcallers": (
        "There's a band of Kolkar Stormcallers at the far side of the oasis, drawing lightning out of a sky that hasn't seen rain in months. It isn't working, and I'd like to lead with that so you go in at the right level of alarm. Whatever they're calling, it isn't coming.\n\nI've watched eleven days of it, and the most they've managed is to make the air taste like a struck anvil. It's still giving every creature within a mile a headache, mine included, and I've started losing the thread of sentences halfway through them, which in a scout is no joke.\n\nThey stay together in the middle, and the dervishes ring them. Break the ring, then the casters. And don't stand in the lightning. I mean that literally.",
        None,
        "The sky's quiet, and I can hear the wind again. I'd forgotten it had a sound of its own.\n\nTake this from the camp's stores.",
    ),
    "nb_verog_the_dervish": (
        "Verog leads them, and I've spent longer than I'd like working out what he really is. He's fast and patient, and quick to take offence, which sounds like a weakness and is the opposite.\n\nThat's why the Kolkar put him here. A man who can be goaded is the finest bait a warband could ask for, because every hero who comes through this oasis thinks his temper is the way in. They get him angry, he spins, and they die in the spin. I've buried enough of those heroes, and I'd rather not bury another.\n\nSo kill the Kolkar Dervishes round him first. You want him alone, with the room empty when he turns. He spins when he's angry, and he's always angry. Wait for the spin to wind down, then hit him.",
        None,
        "Verog's really dead? I never thought I'd see it, and I've been here long enough to stop saying things like that out loud.\n\nTake this, and let me be the first to buy you a drink.",
    ),
    "nb_raiders_at_the_gate": (
        "I'm Outrider Kelsara. Far Watch holds the eastern road, and the Kolkar know exactly what that means, which is more than I can say for half of Orgrimmar.\n\nEvery dawn another band rides up the headland, circles the tower, tests the palisade and goes. That isn't an attack, it's a survey. I've counted forty since the last full moon. I kept the tally on the gatepost until somebody painted over it, and then I kept it in a book. Somewhere in there, I stopped counting them as people and started counting them as a number going up, and I haven't decided what to do about that.\n\nThe Kolkar Raiders test the weak spots, and there aren't any. Convince them of that with a spear.",
        None,
        "There's been nothing at the palisade for an hour now, which is almost suspicious.\n\nTake this, and don't question luck.",
    ),
    "nb_war_pennants": (
        "Every Kolkar warband carries a pennant, a strip of dyed hide on a stick that tells the others who's giving orders. Take enough of them off the field and the bands lose their way, not permanently, but long enough to matter.\n\nThat's the practical reason, and it's a good one. Here's the real one. I've been reading the same colours at the same hour every morning for weeks, in the same order, with the same gap between them and the same band on the left. I can tell the time by which pennant's on the headland, and that's not a skill a person should have.\n\nBring them in, and don't burn them. I want to read them side by side first, because I've got a suspicion about those colours, and I'd like to be wrong.",
        None,
        "The colours match three different bands. They've joined together, and that's bad.\n\nTake this, and be quick going back out.",
    ),
    "nb_scoutriders_on_the_headland": (
        "I've got two problems, and they're the same problem, which took me longer to work out than I'll admit in writing. Kolkar Scoutriders ride the headland every night, measuring the tower's blind spots.\n\nI haven't got any blind spots. I've walked that ground with a lantern at every hour of the night, and I'd back this tower's sight-lines against anything on the plain. What I've got is Kolkar Drudges stealing off the supply line while the scouts are being watched. That's the arrangement. The scouts are there to be looked at, on purpose, every night, so nobody on this wall looks the other way.\n\nKill the drudges first. They're slow, and the scouts will come down to rescue them, which is the one thing about this whole arrangement I actually respect.",
        "On the headland after dark. Kill the drudges first, and the scouts will come to them.",
        None,
    ),
    "nb_kragg_the_wall_breaker": (
        "Kragg is a warbrand chief who's never met a palisade he didn't want to test. He rides a hide-clad warbeast and brings a ram, and he's brought that ram to four walls that I know of, and three of them are gone. This one has stood ten years, and he's decided that's too long. You watch a thing circle your wall enough times, and you learn what it's thinking.\n\nHe usually comes at dusk. Kill the Kolkar Warbrands first, and then him.\n\nI'll be plain with you about what's at stake. If he reaches this wall with that ram, every one of us behind it finds out together what happens next.",
        "The warbrands mass at Mor'shan Base Camp, far to the north-west, so thin them out there first. Kragg himself comes below this palisade at dusk.",
        "Kragg's dead, and the wall holds.\n\nI'll raise a drink and a banner, in that order. Take this, with the Post's thanks.",
    ),
    "nb_slugs_in_the_reeds": (
        "I'm Dugmok, the warden of the fens, which used to be the quietest posting in the Barrens and isn't any more.\n\nThe Slug Fens were only ever slow, wet and quiet. You could walk the causeway with your eyes shut, and the worst that happened was your boots. Then the slugs got big. Nobody knows why. I've asked three shamans and a goblin, and the goblin wanted paying. They're pale and fat and bigger than a dog now, and they creep across the causeway at night.\n\nMy wardens keep that causeway open, and nobody wants to be the one who explains why a caravan went in and the cargo came out as soup. Cut the Slugs back off it. If they wriggle, hit harder.",
        None,
        "It's quiet, a thick, wet, honest quiet.\n\nTake this for wading in.",
    ),
    "nb_venom_glands": (
        "Now for something I want and won't fetch myself, and I'd rather say that plainly than call it delegation. The fen slugs carry a venom gland in the back, a green sac about the size of a fist on the big ones.\n\nCured right, it makes a first-rate poison. I know how, because my mother knew how, and her hands were steadier than mine have been for about four years. So cut them out whole, and don't pop them. If one goes while you're carrying it, we'll be burying a tauren, and the ceremony's long.\n\nThe next caravan through that causeway is going to want every advantage it can carry.",
        None,
        "These are good clean glands, without a mark on them.\n\nI'll make something up for the next caravan.",
    ),
    "nb_peat_hearts": (
        "Deep in the fens, the peat has started to move. I'd like some credit for how calmly I said that, because it took me a season to manage it.\n\nThe Peat Shamblers are mud, root, and old bog-oak that's been down there since before the Horde crossed the sea, and they shuffle up and down the causeway at night. They don't hunt, but anything on the causeway while they're at it simply gets in the way. At the heart of each one there's a core, and it's warm the way a banked fire is, hours after, and it doesn't go out when you take it out of the thing.\n\nI can burn those through a whole cold winter, and my wardens can stop choosing between firewood and sleep. You'll have to break them for it. Hit the middle.",
        None,
        "They're warm already! You're a wonder.",
    ),
    "nb_old_mire_maw": (
        "There's something at the bottom of the deep fen, and I've spent three years not asking anyone about it. Old Mire-Maw is a crocolisk older than the causeway and wider than a wagon, and my grandfather's people laid that causeway.\n\nI've lost three wardens to it. I knew all three, and I sent all three. The first was an accident, the second was bad luck, and the third was me still calling it bad luck. That's all of my reasoning, and I won't make it sound more strategic than it is.\n\nIt hides in the deep pools at the end of the causeway, with Fenwater Crocolisks round it. Don't stand in the water, and don't stand near the water.",
        None,
        "Is Mire-Maw really dead? I never thought I'd say it, and I've been practising.\n\nCome back, and I'll carve you something out of its tooth.",
    ),
    "nb_rig_crews": (
        "I'm Nix, and I call myself a sludge inspector, which is a title I made up for a job nobody's hiring for.\n\nI worked for the Venture Company for years, and they were good years, honestly. Then I found out what they were doing down in the fen. Now I sit in a rusted shack out there counting crews, and come in to Far Watch to report it. They're drilling day and night, the ground is bleeding black and it's reaching the water, and I've watched the edge of the stain move for eleven months.\n\nI want the Venture Co. Oil Drillers gone. I know most of them, and I trained some of them, and I'm asking anyway. The drillers are the loud ones. Stop the noise, and the guards come out, and the guards are quieter and nastier.",
        None,
        "It's really quiet down there now, and the fen's got a pulse again.\n\nTake this, from a man who knows exactly what it's worth.",
    ),
    "nb_sludge_samples": (
        "Where the oil's pooled, the sludge has started to move. The Sludge Elementals are black, sticky and very angry, and I don't think that last word's a figure of speech. Something down there got poured into enough of itself to start having opinions.\n\nI want samples, not for the fen, which is past arguing with, but for the Company. The Board will say the drilling's clean right up until somebody puts a jar on the table. I've sat in those rooms and watched exactly that happen over something smaller.\n\nTake the samples from the middle of them, where it's purest, which is also where they are. They leave a slick behind them, so watch your footing. The goop doesn't come off, ever. I've still got some.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "nb_supervisor_lugwizzle": (
        "Lugwizzle runs the rig. He screams, and he throws wrenches at people and machines, and at the fen in general when there's nobody closer. And he signs every order that comes off that platform, which is why we're talking.\n\nMost goblins running an operation have someone else do the signing, because signing is how you end up in front of a Board. Lugwizzle never does. Everything that happens down there happens because he put his name to it that morning, so if he stops, the rig stops, and if the rig stops, the fen heals, slowly. Probably not in my lifetime, and I've made my peace with that.\n\nHere's a map to the pumphouse. I drew it from memory, and the memory's good. He's usually in there, shouting. He throws oil, so don't stand in it.",
        None,
        "Lugwizzle's dead, and the rig's quiet.\n\nThis is yours. I owe you a lot more than I'll ever have.",
    ),
    "nb_engineer_whirleygig": (
        "With Lugwizzle gone, the engineer's panicking, and a panicking engineer is a different kind of problem from an angry supervisor. Whirleygig is the one who made those drillers work. Lugwizzle signed the orders, and Whirleygig made the things in them exist.\n\nHe's building something large and noisy in the back of the shop. He's been at it since the pumphouse went quiet, and he hasn't come out. It's going to explode. I know I say that a lot. I've said it about a pump, a cart and a kettle this year alone, and I was right about all three.\n\nHe's got turrets up. Kill the Pumpjack Guards, then him. Whatever you do, don't be standing near him when he trips the switch, because he'll trip it the moment he decides he's losing.",
        None,
        "The thing's dead, the engineer's dead, and this fen will heal in time.\n\nThis is the last thing I own that's worth giving.",
    ),
    "nb_claim_jumpers": (
        "I'm Gruda, the claim surveyor, and before you ask, yes, I've got the paperwork.\n\nThe Boulder Lode was a Horde claim, filed, stamped and witnessed by two people who are still alive and will still say so. Then the Venture Company moved in, and now their miners swing picks on my walls. The paperwork isn't helping. I read the relevant line aloud at the mine mouth to a goblin with a clipboard. He wrote something down and nothing happened, and I'm fairly sure what he wrote was my name.\n\nSo the paperwork stays in my pack now. Go in and clear the Venture Co. Miners out of the lower galleries. They aren't soldiers, and I'd like that said, but they'll hit you with a pick all the same.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "nb_heart_of_the_vein": (
        "Now for something I can only get from inside, and it's what I really came out to the Lode for. The Cavern Rock Elementals in that mine are made of the same rock as the walls, with the same seams, and the walls are laced with crystal all the way through.\n\nSo they carry the vein in them. Break one open and you're holding a core sample nobody had to cut. If I can read those, I can tell you how much the Company has already taken out of my claim to within a season, and that's a number I can put in front of somebody in Orgrimmar who outranks a clipboard.\n\nBreak them open, and you'll find a glowing core in each one. Bring me a handful. A single core is one spot, and a row of them shows the whole vein.",
        None,
        "The vein's rich, richer than they ever let on, and they've taken nearly half of it.\n\nTake this, with my thanks.",
    ),
    "nb_prospector_delvik": (
        "Delvik was my partner for twenty-two years. We filed the Boulder Lode together, with both our names on the stamp, and his hand is on half of the original survey.\n\nHe sold the claim's map to the Venture Company for a promise and a bag of silver, and I've had a long time to decide which half of that insults me more. Now he's in a side adit with a rifle and a bad conscience, and he hasn't come out, and the Company hasn't come to get him either. That's what the promise was worth.\n\nHe's in the side adit off the middle gallery, behind the rubble pile. He shoots first. The Venture Co. Tinkerers keep him company, and once they're cleared, you've done most of the work.",
        None,
        "So he got what he deserved.\n\nI'd have wished him a quieter end, but I'm not sorry, and I've stopped waiting to be.",
    ),
    "nb_foreman_grills": (
        "At the top of the mine, round a vein of blue crystal, Foreman Grills runs the Company's crew. He wears an iron hat and carries a bullhorn, and in his own estimation he's the cleverest man ever to hold a pick. He told me so himself, not as a boast, the way you'd mention where you were born.\n\nHe's the loudest man ever to hold a pick, I'll grant him that. But he's been working that blue seam for four months and taking it out in the wrong order, and anybody who'd actually read a vein would know it, and nobody up there will tell him. That's how the whole claim gets ruined even if we win.\n\nHe's in the crystal chamber at the top, and the Venture Co. Overseers hold the way up. Kill them, and he's alone, if you can call a man with a bullhorn alone.",
        None,
        "Grills is dead, the claim's ours, and the vein will outlast every one of us.\n\nTake this, with the claim's honour.",
    ),
    "nb_siege_lines": (
        "I'm Outrider Dask. Mind the guy-ropes, because half this camp is held up by rope and optimism.\n\nMor'shan Base Camp sits under the Rampart like a thorn in the Kaldorei's side. That isn't me being poetic. It's the camp's whole purpose, and it was explained to me in exactly those words. So the Kolkar Warbrands surround it, and I don't blame them. If somebody put a camp under my hill to be a thorn, I'd go and remove the thorn before breakfast. I said that out loud to my own officers, and it didn't go well.\n\nWhat I'd like is simpler than a war. Get them off the supply road. The warbrands are tough and loud, and they always move in fours. Break one group, and the rest will think twice.",
        None,
        "The supply road's clear, or clearer, anyway.\n\nTake this, from a grateful outrider.",
    ),
    "nb_break_the_scouts": (
        "The Kolkar Scoutriders work the hills above this camp and report every movement to the Rampart. I don't mind being watched. The last person I sent up there went in angry about the principle and came back with an arrow in him. We watch them, and everyone out here watches everyone, and nobody's ever died of it.\n\nI mind being shot at, which they do for sport, from a range where it can't possibly be about the camp. That part has nothing to do with the war.\n\nThey shoot from a distance and run. Chase the slow ones. The fast ones will come back on their own, because they can't stand not knowing how it finished.",
        None,
        "They've stopped watching us, and I can have a quiet breakfast for once.",
    ),
    "nb_scout_captain_vrek": (
        "Vrek commands the Rampart-watchers, and he's the one who tells the Kaldorei when to shoot. That's his whole job, and it's worth more to them than any four warbands. A hundred riders can see a thing, but somebody has to decide it matters and say so, and that somebody is Vrek.\n\nHe rides with a sharp-eyed, trained hawk, and between the pair of them there's nothing on this plain they haven't looked at. I want him blind, preferably permanently.\n\nHe hides among the Kolkar Raiders and the scoutriders, which is good sense and makes the fight awkward. If you can reach him, he's weak, because he's a pair of eyes and not a warrior. If he reaches you, you're the weak one.",
        None,
        "Vrek's dead, and the Rampart can't see us for a day or two.\n\nThat may be all we need.",
    ),
    "nb_pickets_past_the_wall": (
        "I'm Captain Korvash. I've come down off the Rampart for an hour, and I'll be back up on it by dark.\n\nThe Rampart is the Horde's wall against the Kaldorei, and here's what nobody puts in a dispatch: it's a wall with a very thin side, and the strip just north of the gate is it. Sentinel Pickets hold the strip and Sentinel Archers cover them from the cliffs, and between them they shoot anything that comes within a hundred paces of our own gate. We aren't holding a wall, we're living behind one.\n\nI've been polite about it for two seasons, and I've sent letters. I'm done being polite. Clear the strip, and take the archers first if you can. Don't be proud about the order. Proud is how the last two went.",
        None,
        "The strip's quiet for now.\n\nThey'll send more. They always send more.",
    ),
    "nb_signal_horns": (
        "Every sentinel picket out there carries a signal horn. One blast and the whole forest answers, not just a patrol. I've heard it once, from the Rampart gate, and I've organised two seasons of orders around never hearing it again.\n\nI want them off the field before the Kaldorei work out how thin this Rampart really is. That's the real position, and I'd rather you knew it going out. We aren't holding them back. We're holding a line they haven't yet decided to test properly, and a horn is how the deciding would start.\n\nCut the horns off the Sentinel Pickets' belts, quickly, before they get them up, and bring them to me. I want to count them, because the count's the only measure I've got of how much time we've bought.",
        None,
        None,
    ),
    "nb_moonspeakers": (
        "Behind the pickets stand the moonspeakers, priestesses who call light down on anyone who falls. They're the reason I've killed the same sentinel three times.\n\nThink about that for a moment, because it took me most of a season to understand what it was doing to my own people. My grunts stopped believing their work counted. You put a picket down, you watch her stand up again, and the next time you go in a little slower, not out of fear, but because nothing you do out there stays done. That's what's really holding this line, not the archers. Take the moonspeakers out, and it breaks.\n\nThe Sentinel Moonspeakers stand at the back, glowing. Close on them fast, because they'll run if you give them time to decide to.",
        None,
        "The line's breaking, and that's what we've been waiting for.\n\nTake this, with the Rampart's thanks.",
    ),
    "nb_warden_ilthae": (
        "Their commander at the Rampart is Warden Ilthae. She's old and steady and silver-haired, and she hasn't lost a skirmish in twenty years. I respect her. I'd like that said once, plainly, and then not again.\n\nShe doesn't waste her people. Every time I've pushed at that strip, she's given me exactly enough ground to make the push expensive and not a step more, and she's never chased me when chasing would have cost her. That's a better officer than I am. I'd still like her dead, because she's what stands between the Horde and this road, and respect was never the same as agreement.\n\nShe's behind the Sentinel Pickets, with a glaive, a bad temper and no fear at all. Don't fight her in the open.",
        None,
        "Ilthae's dead, and the strip is ours.\n\nTake this, and may this wall hold a little longer than she thought it would.",
    ),
    "nb_feathers_on_the_wind": (
        "I'm Zan'ula, so keep walking while we talk, because I don't stand still in these hills, and you shouldn't either.\n\nThe Dry Hills belong to the Witchwings. That isn't me giving anything up. It's a tracker's statement of fact, and the sooner you accept it, the longer you'll live up here. They're harpies, and they've learned a few tricks from somewhere dark. I've tracked harpies my whole life, and these ones do things the others don't.\n\nThe Witchwing Harpies fly low over the ridges and strike from behind, every time, and knowing it doesn't help as much as you'd hope. Keep your back to a hill, and you'll be fine.",
        None,
        "It's quiet in the hills now, for a day or so.",
    ),
    "nb_hexed_totems": (
        "The Witchwing Windcallers carve hex-totems and hang them up in the high rocks. I don't know what they do, and I'd rather admit that than invent a reason, because every tracker I've met who invented a reason ended up believing it.\n\nHere's what I've got. When I camp near them, I dream badly. I'd take nightmares, because I've had those since I was a girl. These are bad in a different way. I wake up having decided things. Twice now I've woken up with a plan I didn't make, and both times it meant going further into those hills.\n\nSo I'd like them down. Pull them off the windcallers, and don't look at them for long. That's rich from a woman who's just admitted she doesn't know what they do.",
        None,
        "They feel wrong even in a bag.\n\nI'll burn them tonight.",
    ),
    "nb_hillcrest_stalkers": (
        "Something else has been working the trail between the hills, and it isn't harpies. The Hillcrest Stalkers are patient and grey, and cunning in a way that makes a hunter uneasy. They don't ambush where the ground is good for ambushing. They ambush where you stop looking.\n\nI've watched one let a traveller pass under it twice before taking him on the third day. That isn't hunger, it's a schedule. I want the trail quiet, and I want the pelts, because grey stalker fetches real coin at the Crossroads, and I'm not too proud to say so.\n\nThey wait on the high ledges, so look up before you walk under any ridge. It gets tedious, and then one day it doesn't.",
        None,
        "These are good pelts.\n\nI'll sell what we don't need at the Crossroads.",
    ),
    "nb_serena_bloodfeather": (
        "Serena Bloodfeather leads the Witchwings. She has an old blood-feud with the Horde and a long memory, and I've stopped asking which came first. I've tracked her for years. I know her roosts and her hours, the three ridges she favours in wind and the one she uses in rain, and I've never once got close.\n\nHere's why, and it's the only useful thing I've got. She doesn't come at the start of a hunt. She comes at the end, when you've been at it long enough to be tired and pleased with yourself. She isn't hunting you. She's hunting whoever you are after an afternoon.\n\nKill the Witchwing Slayers first, always. If she sees you before you're ready, run to the nearest ridge and fight her there. Don't fight her in the open, and don't fight her tired.",
        None,
        "Serena's dead, and the hills are quiet. I can't believe it. I've said her name every day for six years, and now there's nothing on the other end of it.\n\nTake this, with a hunter's thanks.",
    ),
    "nb_roost_raiders": (
        "I'm Rohna, an earthcaller. I speak with the spirits, which out here mostly means I listen to them complain.\n\nSerena's Hollow is a cliff-gorge that winds up to a harpy roost, and the harpies who live in it don't love visitors. That's the ordinary part, and it isn't why I've asked you here. The roost is crying out. I can feel it from the mouth of the gorge, through the stone, the way you'd feel a drum two rooms away. It's pain, or something close enough to it, and the earth has been carrying it for months.\n\nGo in through the cave mouth. The Roost Bonepickers hold the lower nests. They're small and mean and unreasonably fond of the taste of tauren, which I mention as a warning and not a joke.",
        None,
        "That's better, and the earth is calmer.\n\nTake this, because the spirits would want you to have it.",
    ),
    "nb_down_from_the_roost": (
        "Here's something practical while you're in there, and then we'll go back to what's really wrong. The harpies line their nests with down, fine and white and warmer than anything we can weave, and I've sat in the smoke of a tauren loom my whole life.\n\nMy totem-carvers use it in the rites, and they have since before the roost was anybody's. Pull it out of the nests in handfuls, enough to matter, because a pinch does nothing and I'd rather not send you twice.\n\nMind the Harpy Sky-Sentries. They watch from the high ledges, and they're quiet about it.",
        None,
        "It's as soft as a promise.\n\nThe spirits will be pleased.",
    ),
    "nb_windcallers_perch": (
        "High in the gorge there's a perch, and Windcaller Skreeva stands on it. She commands the winds that blow the harpies up and down that cliff, and that's the whole shape of the place. Nothing in that gorge flies on its own strength. The wind carries them, up to the roost and down to the hunt, and she decides when.\n\nThe winds listen to her, and they shouldn't. Wind in a gorge belongs to the gorge. I've asked it directly, and got nothing back but her. I'd rather it listened to someone else, or to nobody at all.\n\nShe's on the east ledge, above the middle of the gorge, and her Harpy Windcallers keep the air thick round her. Kill them first, then her.",
        None,
        "Skreeva's dead, and the winds are quiet.\n\nI can hear them breathing again, just breathing, without being told what to do.",
    ),
    "nb_harpy_matriarch": (
        "At the top of the gorge is the roost itself, a great bowl of nests, hundreds of them, going back further than anyone's counted. In the middle of it sits the Matriarch, mother of every harpy in these hills, and her cry is the one the earth keeps answering.\n\nThat's what I've been hearing since before you came up the gorge, and it's taken me this long to be sure. She isn't calling the harpies, and she isn't calling us. She's calling the ground, every day, and the ground answers. I can feel it answering under my feet in the morning before I'm properly awake.\n\nShe won't leave the nest, and she'll call every harpy in that roost the moment she sees you. Thin the flock first, then end her.",
        None,
        "The Matriarch's dead, and the roost is silent, and the earth with it.\n\nI'll sing the spirits home tonight, and for the first time in a year I know they'll hear me. Take this, with my thanks.",
    ),
    "nb_sprites_in_the_seams": (
        "The ore sprites in the crystal seams are small, bright and mean. They bite anything that gets close, which in a working mine is everything. Gruda would have written them off as vermin, except for one thing. They only live in the rich seams.\n\nShe's looked for months and never found one in a poor seam, and I'd like to know why. A sprite is an assay with legs, and I'd like chips off them for my table.\n\nThey're quick and glowing, and every one of them pauses to sparkle. Hit them in the pause. And don't breathe their dust. Gruda did once, and she tasted copper for a week.",
        None,
        "They're beautiful, and they test out rich too, so Gruda's theory holds.\n\nTake this out of the claim's share.",
    ),
}
